CLASS
A class is a blueprint/template/logical definition that describes
the data and behavior that objects of that class will have.

OBJECT
An object is a runtime instance of a class.

INSTANCE
An instance is a particular object created from a class.

Example:

class Student {
    int rno;
    String name;
    float marks;
}

Student s1 = new Student();

Student → class
s1      → reference variable
new Student() → creates an object
object   → exists at runtime, typically in heap memory


INSTANCE VARIABLES
Non-static fields declared in a class.
Each object gets its own copy.

class Student {
    int rno;
    String name;
    float marks;
}

Each Student object has its own:
rno
name
marks


REFERENCE VARIABLE

Student s1;

s1 is a reference variable.
Initially:

s1 → null


Student s1 = new Student();

Stack/local variable:
s1
 ↓
reference
 ↓
Heap:
Student object
rno = 0
name = null
marks = 0.0


NEW OPERATOR

new Student();

At runtime, new:
1. creates an object
2. initializes its fields with default values
3. returns a reference to that object

That reference is assigned to s1.


COMPILE TIME vs RUNTIME

Student s1 = new Student();

Student s1
→ compiler understands/checks the type and declaration.

new Student()
→ JVM creates the object at runtime.

s1
→ stores the reference to that object.